%I A111291 #12 Nov 09 2026 21:19:31
%S A111291 1,4,16,92,665,5257,44705,394240,3558181,32608999,302172507,
%T A111291 2823898245,26568322474
%N A111291 Number of refactorable numbers (A033950) <= 10^n.
%C A111291 Simon Colton conjectures that the number of refactorables less than x is at least x/(2 log(x)).
%C A111291 Colton also conjectured that pi(n) <= 2*T(n) for all n >= 1, where pi(n) = A000720(n) and T(n) is the number of refactorable numbers <= n. This is true (see the Zenodo link), with equality only at n = 7, and the bound in the previous comment holds for all x > 2. - _Leandre Jack_, Nov 02 2026
%H A111291 S. Colton, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/colton/joisol.html">Refactorable Numbers - A Machine Invention</a>, J. Integer Sequences, Vol. 2, 1999, #2.
%H A111291 Leandre Jack, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23874061">A proof of Colton's conjecture on refactorable numbers</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A111291 Joshua Zelinsky, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL5/Zelinsky/zelinsky9.html">Tau Numbers: A Partial Proof of a Conjecture and Other Results</a>, J. Int. Seq. 5 (2002), Art. 02.2.8.
%t A111291 c = 0; k = 1; Do[ While[k <= 10^n, If[ Mod[k, DivisorSigma[0, k]] == 0, c++ ]; k++ ]; Print[c], {n, 0, 8}]
%Y A111291 Cf. A000720, A033950.
%K A111291 nonn,hard,more,changed
%O A111291 0,2
%A A111291 _Robert G. Wilson v_, Nov 01 2005
%E A111291 a(9)-a(11) from _Donovan Johnson_, Sep 19 2009
%E A111291 a(12) from _Leandre Jack_, Nov 02 2026
